<Doc
  title="Privacy Policy"
  label="Mise · Legal"
  description="What Mise does with your sounds and your information: it keeps them on your devices and in your own Dropbox, and carries what you do between your devices through your own Apple Account."
  lede="Mise has no account with us and no analytics. Your sounds and everything about them stay on your devices and in storage you own. What reaches us is a report when something in the app goes wrong, and, only while you test Mise through TestFlight, the app’s activity log."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <p>
    This policy covers the Mise app for iPhone, iPad and Mac, made by {COMPANY.name} (“we”, “us”), and these pages
    on azorean.io. It applies to nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>We never receive your sounds,</strong> your tags, ratings, notes or recordings. Their names reach us only from TestFlight test builds, in the activity log described below.</li>
    <li><strong>There is no Mise account.</strong> We hold no email, password or profile for you.</li>
    <li><strong>No analytics, no ads, no tracking,</strong> and nothing is sold or shared with advertisers.</li>
    <li><strong>When Mise crashes, freezes or something fails,</strong> it sends us a report through Sentry. It says what failed, where in Mise’s code and on what kind of device. In the App Store version it never holds a sound or its name.</li>
    <li><strong>TestFlight test builds also send the app’s activity log,</strong> which names the files and folders Mise is working on, so we can fix problems while you are testing.</li>
    <li>Your library lives on your devices and, if you connect it, in <strong>your own Dropbox</strong>. What you do to a sound travels between your devices through <strong>your own Apple Account</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where your library lives</h2>
  <h3>On your devices</h3>
  <p>
    Mise keeps an index of your sounds on each device: names, folders, lengths, tempo, key, the families it has
    sorted them into, your marks, crates, likes and notes. Sounds are analyzed on the device itself; the audio is
    never sent anywhere to be analyzed.
  </p>
  <h3>In your Apple Account</h3>
  <p>
    So that your iPhone, iPad and Mac show the same library, Mise keeps what you change, and what it has worked out
    about each sound, in the private iCloud database of your own Apple Account: stars, crates, marks, notes, tempo,
    key, families. Your sound files are not kept there, and Mise does not use iCloud Drive. When your phone plays a
    sound that only your Mac has, the sound passes through that same private database. Only someone signed into
    your Apple Account can read any of it; we cannot. Apple’s privacy policy covers how iCloud stores it.
  </p>
  <h3>In your Dropbox</h3>
  <p>
    If you connect Dropbox, Mise signs in through Dropbox and reads and writes files in your account so your
    sounds can be listed, played and kept in step. The sign-in is stored in your device’s Keychain and, through
    iCloud Keychain, on your other devices. Mise talks to Dropbox directly; nothing passes through us. Dropbox’s
    privacy policy covers your files there. You can disconnect Mise at any time in Mise’s settings or in your
    Dropbox account’s connected apps.
  </p>

  <h2>What Mise asks your device for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microphone</strong> — only to record, and only while you are on the Record page. Recordings are saved into your library.</li>
    <li><strong>Location</strong> — while you use the app, to note where a recording was made. That place is saved with the recording in your library and nowhere else.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos</strong> — only to delete a video from Photos after Mise has taken its audio, and only if you turn that on.</li>
    <li><strong>Local network</strong> — to find your own Mac running Mise, and the devices in a sesh, on the same Wi‑Fi.</li>
    <li><strong>System audio</strong> (Mac) — only to record what an app is playing, while you are on Record and have asked for it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Seshes</h2>
  <p>
    Sesh connects devices near each other, on the same Wi‑Fi. While your Sesh switch is on, anyone nearby with
    Sesh on can see the name you choose, browse your library and your crates, see what you are playing, play your
    sounds and keep copies of them. All of it goes directly between the devices, without passing through us. Turning
    Sesh off, or leaving the app, stops the sharing at once; copies someone kept stay with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem reports and TestFlight</h2>
  <p>
    When Mise crashes, stops responding for a couple of seconds, or something it tries fails (a folder that will not
    list, a sound that will not load, a first setup that shows no sounds after two minutes), it sends a report to us
    through <a href="https://sentry.io">Sentry</a>, a service that collects these reports for developers and keeps them
    on our behalf. A report holds what failed, where in Mise’s code, the device model, the system version, Mise’s
    version, how far the app had got (for example “signed in to Dropbox”, “folder added”), and what it was doing in
    general terms, such as moving to the background. Apple’s own reports on hangs and heavy CPU or disk use are passed on
    the same way. In the version of Mise from the App Store, reports never hold your sounds, their names, folders, tags
    or anything else in your library.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you test Mise through TestFlight,</strong> it also sends us, as it happens, the app’s activity log: the
    lines Mise writes about what it is doing, such as “listed 3,200 sounds in your Samples folder” or “could not load
    Kick 04.wav”, and how long screens and steps take. These lines name the files and folders Mise is working on, and a
    report from a test build carries the last two hundred of them. Email addresses are removed before anything is sent.
    A test build sends at most about six thousand lines a day.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this holds your name, email or IP address, is linked to you, or is used to track you or for anything but
    fixing Mise. Sentry keeps it for 30 days. Sentry’s privacy policy covers how it stores it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Separately, if you have chosen in your device’s settings to share analytics with app developers, Apple may pass us crash
    reports for Mise, which describe the device and what the app was doing when it failed and contain no sounds. Apple
    also shows us totals, such as how many people installed or opened Mise, which never name anyone. If you test Mise
    through TestFlight, Apple shares with us what TestFlight collects and any feedback you send. Apple’s privacy policy
    covers all of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Mise is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect information from them.</p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Because everything is on your devices and in accounts you own, you are in control of all of it. Deleting Mise
    removes its data from that device. Mise’s data in iCloud can be deleted from your device’s settings, under your
    Apple Account’s iCloud storage. Files in your Dropbox stay yours to keep or delete. The only things we hold are problem
    reports and, from TestFlight test builds, activity logs, kept for 30 days and carrying nothing that identifies you.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be posted here with a new effective date. If Mise ever starts
    collecting something it does not today, this page will say so before the app does.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about privacy go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. {COMPANY.name},
    {COMPANY.addressLine}.
  </p>
</Doc>
